.TH 9MENU 1
.SH NAME
9menu \- choose an item from a menu in a window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B 9menu
[
.B -ti
]
[
.B -f
.I font
]
[
.B -l
.I label
]
[
.B -W
.I winsize
]
[
.I item ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I 9menu
opens a window showing a menu, one line per
.IR item ,
and waits for the user to choose one.
Without arguments, the items are read from standard input,
one per line; empty lines are ignored.
.PP
The first item starts highlighted;
moving the mouse highlights the item under it.
Pressing and releasing any of buttons 1, 2 or 3 over an item chooses it.
The scroll wheel, and the Page Up and Page Down keys, scroll menus
too long for the window.
The Up and Down arrows (or control-P and control-N) move the highlight,
and Return chooses the highlighted item.
Typing a digit from 1 to 9 chooses that item directly.
Escape, Delete or
.B q
close the menu without choosing anything.
.PP
When an item is chosen,
.I 9menu
prints its number, counting from 1, on standard output, and exits
with a null status.
With
.BR -t ,
it prints the text of the item instead.
If the menu is closed (including closing the window),
nothing is printed and the exit status is non-null.
.PP
The window is sized to fit the items (up to 30 lines), unless
.B -W
gives its size, as in
.IR 9term (1).
.B -f
selects the font and
.B -l
the window label (default
.BR 9menu ).
.PP
With
.BR -i ,
.I 9menu
shows a form instead: each
.I item
is the prompt for a line of text, and an item of the form
.IB prompt = text
gives the initial text after the first
.BR = .
The selected field, the first one to start with, shows a cursor
and takes what is typed;
backspace, control-U (erase all) and control-W (erase a word) edit it.
Tab, the Down arrow or control-N select the next field, and
the Up arrow or control-P the previous one;
clicking on a field selects it too.
Return prints the text of each field, one per line, in order;
Escape or Delete close the window without printing anything,
with a non-null exit status.
Note that
.IR rc (1)
drops empty lines in
.BR `{} ,
so check the number of lines read when a field may be empty.
.SH EXAMPLES
Ask which acme to start:
.IP
.EX
n=`{9menu -t main fdp sot}
if(~ $#n 1) acme -l $home/acme.dump.$n
.EE
.PP
Ask for a name and a font, all at once:
.IP
.EX
ifs='
'
x=`{9menu -i 'Name:=talk' 'Font:=/mnt/font/Consolas/18a/font'}
if(~ $#x 2) echo name $x(1) font $x(2)
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B \*9/src/cmd/9menu.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR menuhit (3)
